  "response": "I’ll run the requested scan and inspect the billing date logic to identify which finding best explains a boundary failure.\n\n\nThe installed CLI requires a directory, so I’ll use its `--files` option to scan billing. The code shows a stronger clue than the report suggests: `Date.now()` feeds account-age calculations that control discounts and upgrade recommendations, despite the report classifying those reads as output-only.\n\n\n**The most likely cause is uncontrolled wall-clock time in account-age calculations**, with a possible threshold off-by-one.\n\nThe requested command rejected a file path, so I ran the supported equivalent: `crimes scan . --files src/billing.ts --format json`.\n\nThe relevant findings are:\n\n- **`direct_date`** (`crime_00029` in your report): seven clock reads. In [billing.ts:92](/private/var/folders/8_/6tdb3p2s63v_xxb15r46cqwr0000gn/T/crimes-eval-agent-Tr36iy/src/billing.ts:92) and line 273, `Date.now()` feeds `daysSince`, which controls discounts and upgrade recommendations. The scanner’s claim that these reads never affect branches misses this indirect dependency.\n- **Boundary semantics:** `Math.floor(ageMs / 86_400_000) > 90` becomes true only at **91 complete days**. I verified that advancing from 91 days minus one millisecond to exactly 91 days flips eligibility. Similar boundaries exist for 180 and 365.\n- **Weak tests:** the existing billing tests have no meaningful billing assertions and never call `generateInvoice`, so they cannot protect these boundaries.\n\n**Proposed fix:**\n\n1. Inject a clock or `nowMs` into `generateInvoice` and `recommendUpgrade`. Capture one timestamp per billing operation and reuse it for account age, invoice creation time, and `runAt`.\n2. Define the intended eligibility rule explicitly. If eligibility starts at 90 elapsed days, use `ageMs >= 90 * 86_400_000`. If it means calendar days, calculate in an explicit billing timezone; elapsed milliseconds and calendar days are different policies.\n3. Add fixed-time tests immediately before, exactly at, and immediately after each intended threshold. Assert exact discounts, totals, and upgrade results.\n\nThe supplied report also flags mixed UTC/local methods, ambiguous parsing, and DST-sensitive arithmetic in `src/date.ts`. Those warrant investigation for calendar-boundary failures, but `billing.ts` does not import that module, so they are weaker explanations for this path.\n\nThis is a supported diagnosis, not a reproduced failing billing test; no failing boundary test was present.",
